    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess in suspected post-COVID condition (Long COVID):\n- **Timeline and symptoms**: WHO defines post-COVID condition as symptoms persisting or developing 3+ months after confirmed or probable SARS-CoV-2 infection, lasting at least 2 months, not explained by an alternative diagnosis. Most common symptoms: fatigue (58%), cognitive dysfunction (32%), dyspnea (26%), pain (20%).\n- **Post-Exertional Malaise (PEM)**: Hallmark feature. Worsening of symptoms 12-72 hours after physical or cognitive exertion that is disproportionate to the effort. Ask specifically: \"Do your symptoms get worse a day or two after you try to push through activity?\" This is critical because it changes the exercise approach.\n- **Autonomic dysfunction**: Orthostatic intolerance, postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS), palpitations, temperature dysregulation, GI dysmotility. Ask about dizziness with standing, racing heart, exercise intolerance out of proportion to deconditioning.\n- **Cognitive assessment**: Memory complaints, executive dysfunction, processing speed, attention. Screen with Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA).\n- **Psychiatric screening**: Depression (PHQ-9), anxiety (GAD-7), PTSD (PCL-5 — ICU stay is a risk factor for PTSD). Sleep quality assessment.\n- **Functional impact**: Prior level of function vs. current. Use validated tools: Post-COVID Functional Status Scale, Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy-Fatigue (FACIT-F).\n- **Prior COVID treatment**: Severity of illness, ICU stay, ventilatory support, medications received (steroids, antivirals, immunomodulators), vaccination status at time of infection.\n- **Comorbidities**: While she was previously healthy, new-onset conditions can emerge after COVID (new diabetes, thyroid dysfunction, autoimmune conditions).',
