      content: '**Post-COVID Condition (WHO Definition, October 2021)**:\n- Occurs in individuals with a history of probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection\n- Usually 3 months from onset of COVID-19\n- Symptoms last at least 2 months\n- Cannot be explained by an alternative diagnosis\n- Symptoms may be new onset after initial recovery or persist from acute illness\n- May fluctuate or relapse over time\n\n**Differential Diagnosis** (must exclude before attributing entirely to post-COVID):\n\n1. **Cardiac causes of dyspnea/exercise intolerance**:\n- Myocarditis (post-COVID myocardial inflammation — reported in 1-4% of hospitalized patients)\n- Pericarditis\n- Pulmonary embolism (COVID increases VTE risk)\n- New-onset heart failure\n\n2. **Pulmonary causes**:\n- Organizing pneumonia\n- Pulmonary fibrosis (post-ARDS)\n- Pulmonary embolism\n- Reactive airway disease\n\n3. **Endocrine/metabolic**:\n- New-onset hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism (thyroiditis post-COVID is described)\n- Adrenal insufficiency\n- New-onset diabetes mellitus\n- Anemia\n\n4. **Other**:\n- Deconditioning alone (3 weeks of hospitalization causes significant muscle and cardiovascular deconditioning)\n- Depression/anxiety (can cause fatigue, cognitive complaints, and somatic symptoms)\n- Obstructive sleep apnea (can worsen post-COVID fatigue)\n\n**Targeted Workup**:\n\n1. **Laboratory studies**:\n- CBC (anemia), CMP (renal/hepatic function), TSH/free T4 (thyroid), HbA1c (new diabetes), CRP/ESR (residual inflammation), ferritin (elevated in post-COVID, also iron status), D-dimer (if PE suspected), BNP/NT-proBNP (heart failure), cortisol (if adrenal insufficiency suspected)\n\n2. **Cardiac workup** (critical given dyspnea and tachycardia):\n- **ECG**: Assess for arrhythmia, ST changes, prolonged QTc\n- **Echocardiogram**: Assess EF, wall motion abnormalities, pericardial effusion, right heart strain (suggestive of PE or pulmonary hypertension)\n- **Cardiac MRI**: If myocarditis suspected (elevated troponin, new wall motion abnormality, chest pain). Can detect myocardial inflammation, edema, and fibrosis. Gold standard for post-COVID myocarditis diagnosis.\n- **Holter monitor**: If palpitations reported — evaluate for arrhythmia burden\n\n3. **Pulmonary workup**:\n- **Pulmonary function tests (PFTs)**: Spirometry, DLCO. Reduced DLCO is the most common PFT abnormality post-COVID (impaired gas transfer).\n- **CT chest** (if PFTs abnormal): Evaluate for organizing pneumonia, pulmonary fibrosis, PE\n- **CT pulmonary angiography**: If D-dimer elevated or high clinical suspicion for PE\n\n4. **Autonomic testing**:\n- **Active standing test** (done — positive for POTS)\n- **Tilt-table test**: Confirmatory if needed. Monitors HR and BP during 45-degree head-up tilt for 10-45 minutes.\n\n5. **Cognitive testing**:\n- **Neuropsychological testing**: Formal evaluation if MoCA is abnormal or cognitive complaints are significantly impacting function. Can quantify deficits in processing speed, attention, memory, and executive function.',
