      content: '**Critical Principle**: Traditional graded exercise therapy (progressive overload) can be HARMFUL in patients with post-exertional malaise. The rehabilitation approach must be modified to respect the PEM threshold.\n\n**Exercise Management — Pacing-Based Approach**:\n\n1. **Identify the PEM threshold**: Start with very low-intensity activity and monitor for symptom exacerbation 24-48 hours later. Her 6MWT triggered PEM the next day, so her current threshold is below that effort level.\n\n2. **Activity pacing (\"stop-rest-pace\" strategy)**:\n- The \"energy envelope\" concept: total daily activity must stay within 70-80% of the PEM threshold.\n- Alternate activity periods (15-20 minutes) with mandatory rest periods (10-15 minutes).\n- Use a heart rate monitor to stay below the PEM trigger HR. Starting target: stay below **HR 110 bpm** during activity (approximately 15-20 bpm below the HR where she developed PEM symptoms).\n\n3. **Exercise prescription (START LOW, GO SLOW)**:\n- **Phase 1 (Weeks 1-4)**: Supine or recumbent exercises only (recumbent bike, supine exercises, aquatic therapy). 5-10 minutes per session, 3-4 days/week. Intensity: RPE 2-3/10 (very light). NO post-exercise symptom exacerbation is the goal.\n- **Phase 2 (Weeks 5-8)**: If Phase 1 tolerated without PEM, gradually increase to 15-20 minutes. Begin upright activities (seated bike, slow walking).\n- **Phase 3 (Weeks 9-12+)**: Gradual transition to upright exercise. Increase duration before intensity. Goal: 20-30 minutes of low-moderate activity.\n- **Key rule**: If PEM occurs, reduce activity by 50% for the next week, then reattempt previous level. Never push through a PEM episode.\n\n**POTS/Dysautonomia Management**:\n\n1. **Non-pharmacologic**:\n- Increase fluid intake to 2-3 liters/day\n- Increase sodium intake to 3-5 grams/day (if no hypertension or heart failure)\n- Compression garments (abdominal binder > compression stockings for POTS)\n- Avoid prolonged standing, hot environments, and large meals\n- Sleep with the head of bed elevated 10-15 degrees (reduces nocturnal diuresis)\n- Counter-pressure maneuvers (leg crossing, calf muscle pumping) when symptomatic\n\n2. **Pharmacologic** (if non-pharmacologic measures insufficient):\n- **Fludrocortisone 0.1-0.2 mg daily**: Volume expansion. Monitor for hypokalemia and supine hypertension.\n- **Midodrine 5-10 mg TID**: Peripheral vasoconstrictor. Avoid within 4 hours of bedtime (supine hypertension).\n- **Low-dose propranolol 10-20 mg TID**: If hyperadrenergic (resting tachycardia, palpitations). Controls HR without excessive blood pressure reduction.\n- **Ivabradine 5-7.5 mg BID**: Selective sinus node inhibitor. Reduces HR without affecting blood pressure. Emerging evidence in post-COVID POTS.\n\n**Cognitive Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Compensatory strategies**: External memory aids (phone reminders, written lists, structured routines), reduce multitasking, break complex tasks into smaller steps.\n2. **Cognitive exercises**: Computer-based cognitive training programs (attention, processing speed, working memory). 20-30 minutes daily.\n3. **Sleep optimization**: Poor sleep worsens cognitive dysfunction. Sleep hygiene counseling. Consider melatonin 3-5 mg if insomnia present. Rule out sleep apnea if not already done.\n4. **Speech-language pathology referral**: For structured cognitive-linguistic therapy if cognitive deficits are significantly impacting function.\n\n**Fatigue Management**:\n- Energy conservation and activity modification training (OT-led)\n- Structured daily routine with planned rest periods\n- Avoid \"boom-bust\" cycling (overdoing on good days, crashing for days after)\n- Consider low-dose naltrexone (LDN) 1.5-4.5 mg at bedtime (emerging evidence for neuroinflammation-driven fatigue, off-label)\n\n**Mental Health**:\n- Validate the psychological impact of functional loss\n- CBT for illness adjustment, anxiety management\n- PTSD screening (PCL-5) given ICU stay\n- Consider SSRI if depression or anxiety is clinically significant',
